[00:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Your coding agent can run without the cloud. Magnitude profiles your hardware, picks the best local models it can actually handle, and swaps your agent onto them. Free, private, and fully offline. It works with Claude Code, Open'

[00:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Code, Codeex, OpenClaw, and four more harnesses, and it just crossed 3,600 stars in under three months. Here\'s how it works, and who should actually switch. Magnitude fills a gap everyone'

[00:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'running agents has felt. The harnesses are excellent now. We compared six of them last week, but they all assume a cloud API on the other end with token bills, rate limits, and your code'

[00:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'leaving the building. Magnitude is an open-source inference server that sits underneath the agent you already use. It doesn\'t just run models. It figures out which models your specific machine can'

[00:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'run well, tunes the engine for your chip, and rewires your harness to point at local host. And its answer to why not just use Olama is the spiciest FAQ in open source right now. That\'s coming at'

[01:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the end. The project is young and moving fast. Created June 12th of this year, it\'s already at 3,667 stars and 260 forks. Written in Typescript under Apache 2.0. 0. It ships'

[01:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'as an npm CLI, supports Mac OS and Linux natively with Windows through WSL, and there\'s no fixed hardware minimum. The whole point is that it meets your'

[01:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'machine where it is. More memory simply unlocks bigger models. It\'s also refreshingly small in scope. One server, one CLI, one job. The anti-platform in a season of platforms, which is probably'

[01:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'why the self-hosting crowd took to it immediately. The feature list reads like someone got tired of guessing. Magnitude profiles your chip, memory, and bandwidth, then recommends modas with estimated tokens per second before you'

[01:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'download anything. You know how fast it\'ll be in advance. The inference engine is tuned end to end for your machine. Speculative decoding, concurrency, quantization match to what actually fits. models load on demand and'

[02:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'unload when they\'re idle or memory gets tight, so your laptop isn\'t panned at 100% between tasks. After setup, it\'s fully offline, and the bill is zero. No tokens, no API keys, no rate limits'

[02:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'forever. The site makes the pitch in one scroll, the agent you already use, the models your machine can run, and nothing leaving your laptop. What I like most is the honesty of the framing. Magnitude'

[02:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'never claims local models beat the frontier. It claims something more useful for the everyday agent loop. Reading files, writing boilerplate, running commands. A well-tuned local model is fast enough, free, and private.'

[02:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'You keep a Frontier key around for the hard problems and stop paying cloud prices for the routine ones. The integration list is basically our harness wars video in bullet form. Magnitude plugs into Pi, Open Code,'

[02:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Hermes, OpenClaw, Codeex, Claude Code, Oh My Pi, and Klein. And if you don\'t run any of those, it ships a built-in harness of its own. That breath matters because it makes magnitude a neutral'

[03:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'layer. Switch harnesses next month and your local model setup comes with you. The onboarding even writes the harness config for you. So, switch my agent to local models genuinely is one prompt away. It\'s the same neutrality play open'

[03:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'code made on the model side applied one layer down the stack. Own the interface everyone shares and every harness war becomes someone else\'s problem. Setup is genuinely two commands. Install the CLI globally from npm. Then run magnitude'

[03:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'setup. The interactive flow profiles your hardware, shows you the recommended models with their estimated speeds, downloads the ones you pick, and connects them to your harness. There\'s an even lazier path. Paste the setup'

[03:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'prompt from the readme into your agent. And the agent installs Magnitude, runs the onboarding docs, and switches itself over to local models. The tool that convinces your agent to stop calling the'

[04:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'cloud is delivered by the agent itself. The recommendations here are examples from my machine. Yours will differ because that\'s the entire point. The catalog is computed from your hardware, not copied from a blog post. The'

[04:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'momentum is real for a project this young. 3 months from first commit to 3,600 plus stars, 260 forks, an active Discord, and a spot in the default toolkit conversation for every harness'

[04:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'we\'ve covered. Local inference has been possible for 2 years. What changed is that it\'s now organized. One server, eight agent integrations, and zero configuration guesswork. And here\'s the FAQ I promised. magnitude versus just'

[04:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'asking your agent to set up Olama. Their answer nearly verbatim. Your agent would be guessing. It doesn\'t know your hardware, which quantization fits, or how fast it\'ll run. Olama gives you a'

[05:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'model runner. Magnitude gives your agent a catalog with recommendations computed for your machine. An onboarding flu that writes the harness config and inference built for agent workloads. Just in time'

[05:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'loading, idle unloading, memory pressure handling, Olama walked. So this could sprint. But for agent use specifically, the difference between a generic runner and a tuned self-configuring one is the'

[05:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'whole product. So who should actually do this? If you\'re on a beefy laptop and your agent mostly does routine work, file edits, refactors, tests, scripting, local models handle it and the savings'

[05:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'are real. If you\'re in a privacy sensitive or airgapped environment, this is the only way agents happen at all. And if you\'re sensitive, a hybrid setup, local for the loop, frontier for the hard calls, cuts the bill dramatically.'

[05:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Be honest about the trade. Local models still trail the frontier on hard reasoning, and Magnitude doesn\'t pretend otherwise. But two commands in 0 is the cheapest experiment in AI right now.'

[06:05] [scene:talk]
